    "7687249ee2cc62f9f3c1",
    "171819c34465010db75d"
   ],
   "duration": 43.151,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "So here's what an idempotency key is actually buying a client that has no way to know whether its own last request landed: not certainty about the past, but a guarantee about what happens next.",
     "start": 0.15,
     "end": 15.27
    },
    {
     "text": "The server can't tell the difference between a lost response and a lost request either, which is exactly why a stored answer, not a fresh guess, is the only safe thing to hand back on a retry.",
     "start": 15.69,
     "end": 29.354
    },
    {
     "text": "And the hardest part was never the duplicate that arrives late; it was the one that arrives while the first copy is still mid-flight, which is why catching that overlap matters as much as remembering the answer afterward.",
     "start": 29.774,
     "end": 42.801
    }
   ],
